From d62a8e13cd2e4dbed3491de9c9becc5a39bdd68b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <tristan75015@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 06:41:38 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] Heat transfer by kernels: review (all components tested, skew
 conductivity)

- HeatTransferKernelsTest imposes a third temperature on y = 1: the
  solution depends on all the coordinates, so that the y and z components
  of the gradients, of the inner forces and of the derivative of the heat
  flux with respect to the temperature are tested (they were not before).
- New cases: plane stress (the isotropic test behaviour now supports all
  the hypotheses), an anisotropic behaviour with material axes rotated
  differently for each material, in 3D, plane strain and plane stress,
  and two materials (isotropic and anisotropic). The unit square is not
  refined any more (900 elements) to keep the test short.
- The anisotropic test behaviour, OrthotropicStationaryNonLinearHeatTransfer,
  has a skew-symmetric conductivity: the derivative of the heat flux with
  respect to the temperature gradient is not symmetric, so that its
  transposition in the assembled tangent and in the tangent applied
  without assembly is detected (neither this test nor BatchIntegrationTest
  detected it, all their tangent operators being symmetric).
- The heat transfer behaviours are recognised by their structure (one
  vector gradient, one vector thermodynamic force, derivatives with
  respect to the gradient and to the temperature), as the heat transfer
  behaviour integrators do, not by the glossary names of their variables.
- setExternalStateVariablesFromUnknownsValues checks that the external
  state variable is set up and the number of values (generator and the
  six heat transfer behaviour integrators).
- The sequential variants of HeatTransferKernelsTest, which use UMFPack,
  require SuiteSparse.

Mutations of the kernels (y and z components of the gradients, of the
inner forces, of the derivative of the heat flux with respect to the
temperature in the matrix and in the action, transposition of the
derivative with respect to the gradient in the matrix and in the action)
and of the copy of the temperature (plane stress, anisotropic 3D) are all
detected.
